# CASA scenario questionnaire for tethering — how to prepare

> What the written scenario questionnaire looks like and how to prepare for it.
> This file covers **topics and methods only** — it does not publish CASA's
> questions or an answer key, because the point of the questionnaire is that you
> demonstrate your own understanding. Check the current Part 101 MOS before
> relying on any clause number.

Last updated: 22 Nov 2026

---

## What it is

- After the inspector's first review of the Operations Manual, CASA sent a short
  set of **scenario-based questions** to "demonstrate your understanding of the
  applicable rules", with a due date about a week away.
- It was **written, not an interview**. Answers go in a table, with working shown
  where asked, and are returned with the revised manual.
- CASA then came back with a **short follow-up round** on three answers. All
  three were about *evidence*, not the rules themselves: cite the MOS clause
  that states the height limit, give a second data source for an elevation, and
  re-verify a distance. Put those in the first submission and you may save a
  round.

---

## Topics covered

1. **Tether length at several named sites** — site elevation from a mapping tool,
   aerodrome (ARP) elevation from ERSA, the calculation with working, and
   whether the operation can be conducted at all (one site is designed to come
   out as "no").
2. **Distance from the runway centreline** — the minimum perpendicular distance
   at which tethering is permitted.
3. **Why the calculation exists** — a question that gives a take-off point
   height above the aerodrome and asks how long the tether may be and why the
   calculation is necessary.
4. **Military controlled aerodromes** — the additional requirements.
5. **Approach and departure diagrams** — how to read them and how you identify
   approved areas (drone safety apps, aerodrome charts, mapping overlays).
6. **NOTAMs** — lead time and where the request goes.
7. **Take-off height data source** — how the RPA measures height.
8. **Risk assessment procedure** — what is done before a tethered operation.

---

## How to answer well

- **Work from your own manual.** CASA is checking that your procedures and your
  answers agree. Quote your manual section where it helps, and make sure it says
  the same thing as your answer.
- **Cite the MOS clause in every rule-based answer**, for example the tether
  limit (4.04(3)(a)), the height limit (4.04(3)(b)), the black/grey rule
  (4.04(3)(f)) and the geometry (4.05). See the
  [MOS clause quick reference](MOS%20clause%20quick%20reference.md).
- **Show the working** for tether length, in feet, with the elevation source
  named — and apply the **150 ft cap**.
- **Use two elevation sources** for each site and say which one you rely on and
  why. If a later check changes a figure, say what changed and why, and update
  every document that shows it.
- **Attach evidence**: screenshots of the elevation readings (with the
  coordinates visible), the ERSA entry for the aerodrome reference point, and
  the MOS clause text.
- **Read the question literally.** "Minimum distance perpendicular to the
  runway" asks for a distance from the centreline, not the total width of the
  black rectangle.

---

## Common mistakes

| Mistake | Why it matters |
|---|---|
| Giving the formula result without the 150 ft cap | When the site is below the aerodrome the formula can exceed 150 ft, but the tether is limited to 150 ft (4.04(3)(a)). |
| Mixing metres and feet | Elevation tools often show metres; the formula is in feet. |
| Reading dimensions off the MOS figure | The figure only illustrates; use the text of 4.05. |
| Answer says one thing, manual says another | A mismatch between manual and answers is exactly what a reviewer looks for. |
| Citing a form or approval the MOS doesn't require | Check each "approval required" statement against 4.03(2) and 4.04. |
| One elevation source only | Mapping tools can disagree for the same point. |
| Stale cross-references | After restructuring the manual, check every section number you cite. |
| Typos in an answer | Proofread — these are the document CASA keeps on file. |

---

## Process tips

- Treat the questionnaire as a **dry run of your own procedure**: if you can't
  answer from your manual, the manual probably has a gap.
- Return the answers **together with the revised manual** in one email, and list
  what changed in the Amendment Record.
- Keep a copy of the submitted answers and evidence with the approval records.
